WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:03.380
Hey everybody, I'm David Okun with IBM Cloud,

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.780
and today we're gonna talk about Helm.

00:00:05.780 --> 00:00:08.020
Helm is a package manager for Kubernetes

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:11.100
that makes it easy to take applications and services

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.620
that are highly repeatable, or get used in a lot of different scenarios,

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:16.420
and it makes it easier to deploy them

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:18.048
to a typical Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:18.048 --> 00:00:20.400
To really understand and take that home,

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.420
let's walk through a typical scenario

00:00:22.420 --> 00:00:24.620
of a couple of different applications and services

00:00:24.620 --> 00:00:26.740
as they're deployed in Kubernetes.

00:00:26.740 --> 00:00:29.560
So, let's say we're working with an e-commerce application

00:00:29.560 --> 00:00:33.169
that's signing up users for a certain shopping platform for the holidays,

00:00:33.169 --> 00:00:35.760
I'm sure you can think of a couple of examples there.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:39.080
And let's say that we've written a Node.js application

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:41.360
that we're going to deploy to our Kubernetes cluster.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:44.880
Here we're going to say that that's marked here with one deployment

00:00:44.880 --> 00:00:46.640
but we have two replicas of this.

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:49.280
This is because this is going to need to be highly available,

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.899
so we have two replicas to handle all the different requests

00:00:51.899 --> 00:00:53.920
that are going to be coming in.

00:00:53.920 --> 00:00:55.940
And then, additionally, underneath this,

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:00.700
we've got a MongoDB database.

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:03.020
This is going to handle

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.020
communications to, and from, each  of our replicas

00:01:05.020 --> 00:01:08.220
just our main Node.js application that we have,

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:12.380
and let's say that we've also written a service as a way to access our application,

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.400
and let's say we've written that as a node port.

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.420
Node port meaning that there's just a one-to-one ratio between

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:20.780
IP's inside of, and outside of, our Kubernetes cluster.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:24.020
So, in order to deploy this kind of application stack,

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:25.320
you need to define this,

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.580
- or one way that you can define this with Kubernetes,

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.380
is writing a couple of YAML files

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.640
to describe what your deployments are going to look like,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.200
what your service is going to look like, etc.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:36.540
And this is a fairly parse down example,

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.640
but let's go through some of the key elements of this here.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.220
With our deployment of our Node.js application,

00:01:42.220 --> 00:01:44.940
let's assume Mongo's there for you know persistence sake.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.100
With our deployments, we know that we're

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:50.020
going to be writing an image of Node and
Mongo.

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.960
So, for example, our YAML file here

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:58.400
might look something like "node/mongo".

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.740
And you might say version one, something like that.

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:04.680
And, again, for the number of replicas that we have for this application,

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:08.400
we know that we need 2 replicas here to, again,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.000
be able to handle that high availability.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:11.980
Now, for our service.

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:14.800
Again, we decided that it was going to be a type of node port,

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:20.320
so we're going to write that here.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.260
And, again, this is a one-to-one ratio

00:02:22.260 --> 00:02:25.400
between IP's outside of our service and inside of it

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.400
to be able to route it to just one service here on our deployment

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.680
because we have two replicas here to mitigate load whenever it comes to our deployment.

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:35.380
And then, let's say for this particular example,

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:37.420
this is going to be served up on Port 8080.

00:02:37.420 --> 00:02:40.480
So, we can write that

00:02:40.480 --> 00:02:43.600
purely inside our "Service.YAML" will file here.

00:02:43.600 --> 00:02:47.820
Now, this all works, and let's say that you wrote this application yourself,

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.540
you wrote these YAML files yourself,

00:02:49.540 --> 00:02:52.100
you're really familiar with the configuration, so

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:53.980
as long as you're still working on this

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.600
it's fairly easy to be able to make changes to it

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.720
and change things as requirements might change.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:02.550
So, let's say the holidays end and you want to go ahead and spin this down.

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:06.180
You know exactly where to find the number of replicas that you need to change.

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.640
But let's say you move on to a different job in your company,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:10.640
and somebody else is stuck managing this.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:13.600
They might not know, even in this simple example,

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.740
where to go to change the number of replicas

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.020
that you have with this particular application.

00:03:18.020 --> 00:03:21.340
So ,what if there was an easier way to manage the configuration

00:03:21.340 --> 00:03:22.920
for your whole application stack

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:24.720
- and separate that logically

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:27.800
from everything you have with your particular templated application.

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:30.600
This is one place where Helm can really help you out.

00:03:30.600 --> 00:03:34.460
Think of Helm as combining two particular components for your whole application stack:

00:03:34.460 --> 00:03:35.680
a configuration,

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.120
which is going to be defined by our values that YAML file up here,

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:45.580
and a template - or what we call in Helm,  a chart.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:49.480
Your chart is going to consist of all the files that you're going to be templating here.

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:51.680
Now how do we actually template these files

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:53.140
and inject variables into them?

00:03:53.140 --> 00:03:55.590
Well, you're going to take your configuration and

00:03:55.590 --> 00:03:59.370
you're going to instead use templating language to decide where those values

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:02.360
are going to be put inside your templates, or your chart, here.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:07.470
So, let's say we want the number of replicas to be determined by our configuration up here

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.720
instead of hard-coding that into our particular "Deployment.YAML" file.

00:04:11.720 --> 00:04:13.140
The way we could change that

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:17.880
is we could say replicas are, instead, going to be managed by

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:25.820
"Values.deployment.replicas",

00:04:25.820 --> 00:04:29.620
(I'll shorten it but you understand you just write after that).

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:31.520
So, that way this is going to say,

00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.340
"OK, we're going to refer to the Values.deployment.replicas node,"

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:38.140
and sure enough, that's much easier to find in our configuration.

00:04:38.140 --> 00:04:40.660
So, we can simply write "2" here.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:44.260
Now, this means that, depending on what you want hard-coded in your template,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:47.080
you can decide whether to keep that hard-coded here,

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:48.810
or you can make another change here,

00:04:48.810 --> 00:04:50.720
and simply refer to the same thing,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:53.860
instead you would say "image",

00:04:53.860 --> 00:05:01.460
and then you could say something like "node/mongo1".

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.340
So, you could do the same thing for your service as well.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:07.420
Let's say you want to go from using a node port to a load balancer,

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:09.380
as given to you by Kubernetes in the future.

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:12.120
So, that you could instead change this,

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:20.480
and you could say "Values.service.type",

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:31.240
and you can change this to say "Values.service.port".

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:33.800
This means that for a developer on this project,

00:05:33.800 --> 00:05:35.480
or for somebody who's working infrastructure,

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.640
and making sure that this is deployed fresh,

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.440
you can simply have them change your configuration here.

00:05:40.440 --> 00:05:44.360
So, for example here we're just gonna write "Node Port",

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:47.260
and for this we'll just choose 8080.

00:05:47.260 --> 00:05:50.540
Now, how does this actually work, and how does this get all of this

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.460
combined into your Kubernetes cluster?

00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:54.340
Well, the command that you're gonna write

00:05:54.340 --> 00:05:56.660
when you install the Helm CLI on your machine,

00:05:56.660 --> 00:05:58.460
if you want to combine this into one chart,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:04.540
you would simply write "helm install" ...

00:06:04.540 --> 00:06:07.180
... something like "MyApp",

00:06:07.180 --> 00:06:10.860
if you're referring to the right folder that you have as the CLI tells you to do.

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:13.640
And what Helm will do, is it will actually

00:06:13.640 --> 00:06:17.040
take the templating chart that you have,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:21.840
look for particular parts where you've defined variables in your configuration,

00:06:21.840 --> 00:06:24.360
and it will go to that configuration file,

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:26.360
use the YAML to find the Nodes that you need,

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:29.820
and inject those parameters inside your templating file.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.700
Once it's got everything comprised,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.180
Helm will send this over

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:35.980
to a component that needs to be installed on your Kubernetes cluster

00:06:35.980 --> 00:06:37.300
called "Tiller".

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:40.440
Think of Tiller as basically just the server-side component of Helm,

00:06:40.440 --> 00:06:43.340
it's going to take the commands you've sent with the Helm client,

00:06:43.340 --> 00:06:46.040
and turn that into something that your Kubernetes cluster will understand,

00:06:46.040 --> 00:06:49.300
so that it will say, "OK, take the service, map that there,

00:06:49.300 --> 00:06:51.000
take the deployment, map that there,

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:53.140
and take this database, and map it there."

00:06:53.140 --> 00:06:54.700
Now, this becomes extra useful

00:06:54.700 --> 00:06:58.280
when you want to do things like upgrade to a new configuration and rollback.

00:06:58.280 --> 00:07:00.640
Let's say you're past the holidays and you say,

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.120
"OK, we can spin this down to where we only need one replica now of this particular deployment."

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:08.740
So, rather than taking down the application whole-meal,

00:07:08.740 --> 00:07:11.860
and then going ahead and redeploying it with the new configuration,

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:19.340
you could simply instead type "helm upgrade MyApp".

00:07:19.340 --> 00:07:23.280
So, you could just replace "install" with "upgrade",

00:07:23.280 --> 00:07:25.260
and that's how you can use that same syntax.

00:07:25.260 --> 00:07:27.760
That way, what you would do is, Helm would, again,

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:30.780
template everything out, make sure that it works the way you want it to,

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:33.220
and get that configuration sent over to Kubernetes

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:36.500
so it can manage uptime as being the most important characteristic

00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:38.180
of your particular stack.

00:07:38.180 --> 00:07:40.280
Let's say you made a mistake when you were upgrading it,

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.720
and something that you just changed it to doesn't work.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:44.180
The way to roll that back

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:49.000
- you guessed it - is the "Rollback" command.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:52.380
What Helm will also give you is it actually keeps

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:56.800
a version history for you of different configurations that you've sent over the wire with Helm,

00:07:56.800 --> 00:07:59.670
so that you can rollback to the last known working configuration

00:07:59.670 --> 00:08:01.140
whenever you want to.

00:08:01.140 --> 00:08:03.810
Lastly, when you're working with these you've talked about,

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:05.640
hey, maybe you moved on to a different
team

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:09.380
- but maybe other teams need this  exact same kind of configuration as well.

00:08:09.380 --> 00:08:11.960
Well, you can make use of Repos as well

00:08:11.960 --> 00:08:16.660
and you can actually deploy your charts
into that Repo

00:08:16.660 --> 00:08:21.120
by typing "helm package"

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:23.900
and then using "helm repo index" to send that up to a repository,

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:27.480
so anyone in your company can make use of the same chart as well.

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:32.020
So, what we've discussed is basically taking a typical scenario,

00:08:32.020 --> 00:08:35.460
going through it in a fairly understandable way by hard coding your YAML,

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:37.600
but, by parameterizing it with Helm,

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:40.360
you just make it a little bit easier to manage these packages,

00:08:40.360 --> 00:08:41.760
and upgrade them when you need to,

00:08:41.760 --> 00:08:45.040
and make it easier for everyone else on your team to understand it as well.

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:48.840
It's currently in the incubation phase of the Cloud-Native Computing Foundation

00:08:48.840 --> 00:08:51.380
and it's ready for open source, and ready for you to use today.

00:08:51.380 --> 00:08:52.240
Thank you.

00:08:52.240 --> 00:08:55.000
If you have questions please drop us a line below.

00:08:55.000 --> 00:08:57.540
If you want to see more videos like this in the future,

